{1}$, …, ${F}_{s}\\in {\\mathcal{F}}_{s}$. We investigate the question, posed by Kupavskii and Popova, of determining the smallest $c=c(n,s,k)$ such that the arithmetic progression $c$, ${n}^{k-1}+c$, $2{n}^{k-1}+c$, …, $(s-1){n}^{k-1}+c$ is satisfying. We prove that the sequence is satisfying if $c\\ge {\\Omega }_{k}(max({s}^{2}{n}^{k-2},s{n}^{k-3/2}\\sqrt{logs}))$, improving the previous result by Kupavskii and Popova. We also study satisfying sequences for $k=2$ using the polynomial method, extending the previous result by Kupavskii and Popova to when $n$ is not prime.\n\n## Introduction\n\nOne of the most basic open problems in extremal set theory is the Erdős matching conjecture [5], which states that if $n\\ge ks$ and $\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{\\left[n\\right]}{k}\\right)$ (see Section 2.1 for notation) satisfies $$\\left|\\mathcal{F}\\right|>t(n,s,k)≔max\\left(\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{sk-1}{k}\\right),\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n}{k}\\right)-\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n-s+1}{k}\\right)\\right),$$then there exist pairwise disjoint sets ${F}_{1},\\dots ,{F}_{s}\\in \\mathcal{F}$. In other words, this conjecture asks whether $t(n,s,k)$ is the maximum number of hyperedges in a $k$-uniform hypergraph without $s$ disjoint hyperedges. Note that the bound $t(n,s,k)$ cannot be improved because neither family $\\mathcal{F}=\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{[sk-1]}{k}\\right)$ nor $\\mathcal{F}=\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{\\left[n\\right]}{k}\\right)\\setminus \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{[n-s+1]}{k}\\right)$ contain such pairwise disjoint ${F}_{1},\\dots ,{F}_{s}$. This conjecture is known to hold when $k=2$ [6, Thm. 4], when $k=3$ [8], and when $n\\ge \\frac{5}{3}sk$ and $s$ is sufficiently large [9, Thm. 1], but the general case remains open.\n\nThus, if a monomial ${x}_{1}^{{e}_{1}}\\cdots {x}_{s}^{{e}_{s}}$ has a nonzero coefficient in ${\\prod }_{1\\le i<j\\le s}{\\left({x}_{i}-{x}_{j}\\right)}^{2}$, then ${e}_{i}={a}_{i}+{b}_{i}$, for some permutations $({a}_{1},\\dots ,{a}_{s})$ and $({b}_{1},\\dots ,{b}_{s})$ of $\\{0,1,\\dots ,s-1\\}$. Furthermore, Theorem 1.7 only works when $n=p$ is prime and require the coefficient to be nonzero modulo $p$ (instead of nonzero).\n\nTheorem 1.6 implies that the sequence $\\left(s-1\\right)n$, …, $\\left(s-1\\right)n$ and the sequence 0, $2n$, $4n$, …, $2\\left(s-1\\right)n$ are both satisfying. Note that these two sequences are far from optimal, especially when $s$ is very small since we know from [13, Thm. 9] that the sequence $c,n+c,2n+c,\\dots ,\\left(s-1\\right)n+c$ is satisfying when $c\\ge 4{s}^{2}$.^{1} The key input for Theorem 1.6 is the multivariate generalization [4] of Alon’s combinatorial nullstellensatz [2].\n\nWe prove Theorem 1.4 in Section 3 and prove Theorem 1.5 in Section 4. The proofs of these two theorems are similar, except that in the proof of Theorem 1.5, one replaces an elementary argument with the method of spread approximation to eliminate the factor of $k$ in the bound. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeometry of multilinear varieties over infinite fields and its applications (https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.04859)\nciteturn12academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; As applications, we resolve the Adiprasito-Kazhdan-Ziegler conjecture [arXiv:2102.03659, 2021] on the stability of partition rank for perfect infinite fields. ... Moreover, our results immediately yield a strengthening of the theorems of Bik-Draisma-Snowden [arXiv:2401.02067, 2024] and Lampert-Snowden [arXiv:2406.18498, 2024], for multilinear varieties over infinite fields.\nTitle: Geometry of multilinear varieties over infinite fields and its applications\nAuthors: Qiyuan Chen, Ke Ye\nDate: Wed May  6 12:56:05 2026\n\nMultilinear varieties, defined as the sets of rational points of varieties cut out by multilinear functions, were first introduced and studied by Gowers and Milićević[Proc. Edinb. Math. Soc., 2021] for finite $\\mathbb{K}$. In this paper, we investigate multilinear varieties over infinite fields from a geometric perspective. We establish two fundamental results: a codimension formula for the Zariski closure of a multilinear variety, and the existence of a high-dimensional irreducible subvariety passing through any given $\\mathbb{K}$-rational point. These results serve as a geometric foundation for analyzing various ranks of tensors and homogeneous polynomials, including partition rank, analytic rank, geometric rank, (collective) strength and (collective) Birch rank. As applications, we resolve the Adiprasito-Kazhdan-Ziegler conjecture [arXiv:2102.03659, 2021] on the stability of partition rank for perfect infinite fields. We thereby settle the stability conjecture for collective strength [Selecta Math., 2024], as well as the conjecture on the linear equivalence between strength and Birch rank [arXiv:2410.00248, 2024] for such fields. Moreover, our results immediately yield a strengthening of the theorems of Bik-Draisma-Snowden [arXiv:2401.02067, 2024] and Lampert-Snowden [arXiv:2406.18498, 2024], for multilinear varieties over infinite fields.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinding an almost perfect matching in a hypergraph avoiding forbidden submatchings - Delcourt - 2026 - Journal of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1112/jlms.70660)\nciteturn12search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; (Grinblat [[30]])If $$G$$ is a multigraph that is (not necessarily properly) edge colored with $$n$$ colors where each color class is the disjoint union of non-trivial cliques and spans at least $$3n-2$$ vertices, then $$G$$ has a rainbow matching of size $$n$$. ... In this paper, we use a simple deletion trick combined with Theorem 2.11 to prove a bounded multiplicity strong asymptotic version of Conjecture 2.13 for hypergraphs in the sparse setting that finds many disjoint full rainbow matchings as follows. ... By choosing $$\\alpha$$ small enough, it now follows from Theorem 2.6 applied to $$H^{\\prime }$$ that there exists a full rainbow matching of $$G$$ and indeed even a set of $$D$$ disjoint full rainbow matchings of $$G$$.\n### 2.5 More applications to rainbow matchings\n\nHere is another well-studied rainbow matching conjecture.\n\nConjecture 2.12. (Alspach [[9]])If $$G$$ is a simple $$2d$$-regular graph that is edge colored such that each color class is a 2-factor, then $$G$$ has a full rainbow matching.\n\nMunhá Correia, Pokrovskiy, and Sudakov [15] proved the strong asymptotic version of Conjecture 2.12. We note though that our Theorem 2.11 immediately implies the strong asymptotic version of Conjecture 2.12 in the sparse setting.\n\nHere is another rainbow matching conjecture, originally motivated by equivalence classes in algebras, which has received much attention.\n\nConjecture 2.13. (Grinblat [[30]])If $$G$$ is a multigraph that is (not necessarily properly) edge colored with $$n$$ colors where each color class is the disjoint union of non-trivial cliques and spans at least $$3n-2$$ vertices, then $$G$$ has a rainbow matching of size $$n$$.\n\nThe strong asymptotic version of Conjecture 2.13 was proved by Clemens, Ehrenmüller, and Pokrovskiy [13] (see Munhá Correia, Pokrovskiy, and Sudakov [15] for a short proof). Conjecture 2.13 was then fully proved by Munhá Correia and Sudakov [16].\n\nBetter bounds are known though when assuming the graph has bounded multiplicity. For example, Munhá Correia and Yepremyan [17] proved the version where if $$G$$ has multiplicity at most $$\\sqrt {n}/\\log ^2 n$$ and each color class spans at least $$2n+o(n)$$ vertices, then $$G$$ has full rainbow matching. Munhá Correia, Pokrovskiy, and Sudakov [15] improved this further by showing that for multiplicity $$m$$, each color class spanning $$2n+2m+O\\left((n/\\log n)^{1/4}\\right)$$ suffices.\n\nIn this paper, we use a simple deletion trick combined with Theorem 2.11 to prove a bounded multiplicity strong asymptotic version of Conjecture 2.13 for hypergraphs in the sparse setting that finds many disjoint full rainbow matchings as follows. See Section 8 for its proof.\n\nTheorem 2.14.For all integers $$r\\geqslant 2$$ and real numbers $$\\beta > 0$$, there exist an integer $$D_{\\beta }$$ and real $$\\alpha > 0$$ such that the following holds for all $$D\\geqslant D_{\\beta }$$:\n\nIf $$G$$ is an $$r$$-uniform (multi)-hypergraph with codegrees at most $$D^{1-\\beta }$$ that is (not necessarily properly) edge colored where each color class is the disjoint union of nontrivial cliques and spans at least $$rD(1+D^{-\\alpha })$$ vertices and each vertex is incident with at most $$D$$ colors, then $$G$$ has a full rainbow matching and indeed even a set of $$D$$ disjoint full rainbow matchings.\n\nFinally, we turn to the third weakening direction where the number of colors is allowed to be slightly larger than as conjectured. Aharoni and Berger [1] and in a slightly weaker form Drisko [18] proved that if $$G$$ is a bipartite multigraph that is properly edge colored with $$2n-1$$ colors each appearing exactly $$n$$ times, then $$G$$ has a rainbow matching of size $$n$$. Barát, Gyárfás, and Sárközy [10] conjectured that this should hold even for nonbipartite multigraphs when $$n$$ is odd (and with $$2n$$ colors when $$n$$ is even). Aharoni, Berger, Chudnovsky, Howard, and Seymour [2] proved $$3n-2$$ colors suffice, and this was improved to $$3n-3$$ by Aharoni, Briggs, Kim, and Kim [3].\n\nChakraborti and Loh also conjectured the following.\n\nConjecture 2.17. (Chakraborti and Loh [[12]])For every real $$\\varepsilon > 0$$, there exists $$q_0$$ such that for all integers $$q\\geqslant q_0$$, the following holds: If $$G$$ is a simple bipartite graph that is properly edge colored with $$(1+\\varepsilon)q$$ colors such that each color appears exactly $$q$$ times, then $$G$$ has a rainbow matching of size $$q$$.\n\nOnce again we can use Theorem 2.11 to prove their conjecture with the following stronger statement as follows. See Section 8 for its proof.\n\nTheorem 2.18.For every real $$\\varepsilon > 0$$, there exists $$q_0$$ such that for all integers $$q\\geqslant q_0$$, the following holds: Let $$r\\leqslant q$$ be a positive integer. If $$G$$ is a simple bipartite graph with at least $$(1+\\varepsilon)qr$$ edges that is properly edge colored with $$(1+\\varepsilon)q$$ colors such that no color appears more than $$q$$ times, then $$G$$ has a rainbow matching of size $$r$$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimona Boyadzhiyska: Almost-perfect colorful matchings in three-edge-colored bipartite graphs | HUN-REN Rényi Intézet (https://www.renyi.hu/node/5003)\nciteturn12search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; 2026. ... The well-known Ryser-Brualdi-Stein Conjecture states that any proper edge-coloring of the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} contains a rainbow matching of size n-1. ... More precisely, we will show that, for all positive integers n,a_1, a_2, a_3 satisfying a_1+a_2+a_3 = n-1, every bipartite graph on 2n vertices that is the union of three perfect matchings M_1, M_2, and M_3 contains a matching M such that |M ∩ M_i| =a_i for i ∈ {1,2,3}.\n\n# Simona Boyadzhiyska: Almost-perfect colorful matchings in three-edge-colored bipartite graphs\n\n2026. 04. 24. 14:15 - 2026. 04. 24. 15:45\n\nKutyás terem\n\nGeoScape\n\nGeometry\n\nEsemény típusa: szeminárium\n\nSzervezés: Intézeti\n\nwebpage\n\nBudapest Big Combinatorics + Geometry Seminar\n\n### Leírás\n\nThe well-known Ryser-Brualdi-Stein Conjecture states that any proper edge-coloring of the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} contains a rainbow matching of size n-1. In this talk, we will discuss a multiplicity extension of this conjecture, proposed recently by Anastos, Fabian, Müyesser, and Szabó, and prove a special case of it. More precisely, we will show that, for all positive integers n,a_1, a_2, a_3 satisfying a_1+a_2+a_3 = n-1, every bipartite graph on 2n vertices that is the union of three perfect matchings M_1, M_2, and M_3 contains a matching M such that |M ∩ M_i| =a_i for i ∈ {1,2,3}. This is joint work with Micha Christoph and Tibor Szabó.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-30 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-10-30)\nciteturn12search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; In this paper, we show that if $n\\ge3$, $e(G_i)> \\binom{n-2}{2}+2$ for each $i\\in[n-1]$ and $G_j$ is connected for some $j\\in[n-1]$, then there exists a rainbow Hamiltonian path in $\\mathbf{G}$ unless $n=6$ with $G_1=G_2=\\dots=G_{5}\\cong K_2\\vee 4K_1$, or $n=8$ with $G_1=G_2=\\dots=G_{7}\\cong K_3\\vee 5K_1$. This corrects Theorem 6 in [Graphs Combin., \\textbf{41}(6)(2025), 119] and relaxes its hypothesis from requiring all $G_i$ to be connected to only requiring that at least one $G_i$ is connected, where $i\\in[n-1]$. ... In this paper, we address the following question: for which sequences $f_1,\\ldots, f_s$ do the conditions $|\\mathcal F_i|>f_i$ imply that the families contain a rainbow matching, that is, there are pairwise disjoint $F_1\\in \\mathcal F_1,\\ldots, F_s\\in \\mathcal F_s$?\ncomplete graph graph random random graph coloring\n\n2026-10-30 v2\n\nAndrey Kupavskii, Elizaveta Popova\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nSatisfying sequences for rainbow partite matchings\n\nLet $\\mathcal F_1,\\ldots, \\mathcal F_s\\subset [n]^k$ be a collection of $s$ families. In this paper, we address the following question: for which sequences $f_1,\\ldots, f_s$ do the conditions $|\\mathcal F_i|>f_i$ imply that the families contain a rainbow matching, that is, there are pairwise disjoint $F_1\\in \\mathcal F_1,\\ldots, F_s\\in \\mathcal F_s$? We call such sequences {\\em satisfying}. Kiselev and the first author showed that $f_1 = \\ldots = f_s=(s-1)n^{k-1}$ is satisfying for $s>470$, verifying the conjecture by Aharoni and Howard. This is the best possible if we insist that the families are of the same size. However, it turns out that much more can be said about asymmetric restrictions. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRon Aharoni - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/ron-aharoni)\nciteturn12search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; In the spirit of a generalization of the CHC to rainbow cycles in \\cite{ADH2019}, this suggests the conjecture that given non-empty sets $F_1, \\ldots,F_n$ of edges of $K_n$, there exists a rainbow cycle of length at most $2\\sum_{1\\le i \\le n}\\frac{1}{|F_i|+1}$. ... Drisko proved that $2n-1$ matchings of size $n$ in a bipartite graph have a rainbow matching of size $n$. For general graphs it is conjectured that $2n$ matchings suffice for this purpose (and that $2n-1$ matchings suffice when $n$ is even).\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nBadges and rainbow matchings\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nDrisko proved that $2n-1$ matchings of size $n$ in a bipartite graph have a rainbow matching of size $n$. For general graphs it is conjectured that $2n$ matchings suffice for this purpose (and that $2n-1$ matchings suffice when $n$ is even). The known graphs showing sharpness of this conjecture for $n$ even are called badges. We improve the previously best known bound from $3n-2$ to $3n-3$, using a new line of proof that involves analysis of the appearance of badges. We also prove a \"cooperative\" generalization: for $t>0$ and $n \\geq 3$, any $3n-4+t$ sets of edges, the union of every $t$ of which contains a matching of size $n$, have a rainbow matching of size $n$.\n\nbipartite graph graph matching\n\n2020-03-18 v2\n\nRon Aharoni, Joseph Briggs, Minho Cho, Jinha Kim\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nCooperative conditions for the existence of rainbow matchings\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nLet $k>1$, and let $\\mathcal{F}$ be a family of $2n+k-3$ non-empty sets of edges in a bipartite graph. If the union of every $k$ members of $\\mathcal{F}$ contains a matching of size $n$, then there exists an $\\mathcal{F}$-rainbow matching of size $n$. Replacing $2n+k-3$ by $2n+k-2$, the result is true also for $k=1$, and it can be proved (for all $k$) both topologically and by a relatively simple combinatorial argument. The main effort is in gaining the last $1$, which makes the result sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelecta Mathematica (2024) 30:15 (https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10535322)\nciteturn12search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Page 9 of 19  15On rank in algebraic closureWe prove theorem 3.4 for all of the above fields (except \\(\\mathbb{Q}\\)) via a universality result, which may be of independent interest. This generalizes a universality result of Kazhdan and Ziegler appearing in [12]. ... To show that the right hand side equals $\\bar r$, we need the following simple claim:Claim 3.6  Let k be a field and \\(P : V_1 \\times \\dots \\times V_d \\to k\\) a multilinear form.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArxiver (https://arxiver.lazybrains.com/author/473002)\nciteturn12search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Created: 2026-06-22 Updated: 2026-06-24\n\n#### Monadic non-definability and gain-graphic matroids\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversality of High-Strength Tensors (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9054888/)\nciteturn12search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; A theorem due to Kazhdan and Ziegler implies that, by substituting linear forms for its variables, a homogeneous polynomial of sufficiently hi